{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 5a9ee59f7293763086d07bcd623e75bbda8f9d48, on planner/actenv-amend4-20261115, base main 53aeda16, its one artifact fb15b70c (`notes/2026-11-14-act-environment.md`), for request e4b9eafc under my promise 3c9b14b0.\n\nWhat I checked, ruling by ruling:\n\n- `34791662`, section 7.1. The four paragraphs follow items 1 to 3 and the storage paragraph. A position names a gesture, and A, B, A makes position 2 with a new deadline that never reuses the envelope of position 0. The descriptor digest is a required option that keeps the value of the first submit. A signing with no reference is `legacy`, and the suite fails on any unseeded one. The authorization write comes first, under one fence.\n- `f494b29d`, section 7.1 and section 15. The mark is written under the fence before the signer runs, and it stays after a signer or custody failure. A second attempt stops with `authorization-used`. The new row of section 15 narrows \"A stop changes no store\", the sentence at line 514 of `notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md`, to custody records, as item 5 and the closing paragraph of the ruling state.\n- `686dc913` and `63f034d9`, section 7.4. The instance has one ordering policy. A selection through a pending source stops before retention and writes nothing, and step E5 owns it.\n- `52b638a9`, section 7.1. The store keeps history and deletes or evicts nothing. A key holds at most 64 positions, counting unsigned and failed ones. A gesture past the bound stops before any write, and the bound is a constant separate from `SIGNINGS_MOST` (`packages/client/src/custody.ts:90`, value 64).\n- `d48ce431`, sections 6, 7.1, 7.4 and 13. The Replica owns the exploration operation and answers `unavailable` without authority. The draft precondition runs under the same fence before the mark and stops with `taken`. The E3 row loses the four items that C4 now owns. The removal rule matches item 6. The E4-B row says \"It lands in the C4 head\". The ruling left that choice to the builder, and the builder's promise `31520bd1` states it.\n- My approval `1e7574bd`, section 16. The paragraph states the absence proof's dependence on admission refusing an act after its `notAfter`, as I wrote it.\n\nStop names. The note names `authorization-used` and `descriptor-mismatch` from the rulings, and `taken` and `unavailable`, which the scope client note and ruling `d48ce431` already use. It names no other stop. I found no sentence that claims more than a ruling states.\n\nTwo points the note leaves open, as the request says:\n\n1. Ruling `52b638a9` item 3 asks for one stop name past the bound of 64. Ruling `63f034d9` item 2 asks for one stop name for a selection through a pending source. The note names neither. The E4 implementation note owes both.\n2. Ruling `d48ce431` item 6 says that if the custody archive removes nothing today, the authorization store removes nothing, and the note says so. Section 7.1 states the removal rule but not what the archive removes today. E4-B item 3 and promise `31520bd1` carry that fact, so the E4-B report owes it.\n\nCheap checks: the commit-message check passes, 1 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. This is a notes-only head, so I ran no tests. I relied on the request for `check-notes` and `check-doc-links`.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The amendment records each ruling in the section it governs and indexes them in section 19, the same way section 18 indexes the E1 rulings.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. One call of the signer per position, a mark that outlives a failure, and the authorization write before custody each make a retry or reload unable to sign twice for one gesture. The admission fact in section 16 keeps the absence proof's dependence visible to later reviews.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nE4 is on a private branch and not on main. This approval covers the note only, and it authorizes no build, landing or deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d5